#%% md
**Takeaway:** «O61»

## 7. Statistical Analysis (core section)

### 7.1 What happens: win rates against a neutral baseline

**(a) Why?** The audit's first step is descriptive: how often do related sellers win, and how often when they are **not the cheapest** (out of position)? A win rate means little alone, so we compare it with a **neutral counterfactual on the same competitions**: "lowest total price (price plus shipping) wins; ties go to faster delivery". Because both rules are applied to the same competitions, the comparison is **paired** and the test is **McNemar**.

**(b) Assumptions:** competitions are independent snapshots (they are repeated visits to products, so there is some clustering by product; we also bootstrap by product); the counterfactual ignores the possibility that sellers would price differently under a different rule.

#### Code

#%% code
n_c = len(win); rel_win = (win.related == 1).values; oop = rel_win & (win.is_min.values == 0)
lp = o.sort_values(["comp_id", "total", "fast", "n_ratings"], ascending=[True, True, False, False]).drop_duplicates("comp_id").set_index("comp_id").sort_index(); rel_lp = (lp.related == 1).values
rows = []
for t_ in T3:
    k = int((win.seller_type == t_).sum()); lo, hi = proportion_confint(k, n_c, method="wilson"); kb = int((lp.seller_type == t_).sum()); rows.append((t_, (o.seller_type == t_).mean(), k / n_c, lo, hi, kb / n_c))
bb = pd.DataFrame(rows, columns=["seller type", "share of offers", "Buy Box win share", "CI low", "CI high", "win share under lowest-price rule"]).set_index("seller type")
a_, b_ = rel_win, rel_lp
mc = mcnemar([[int((a_ & b_).sum()), int((a_ & ~b_).sum())], [int((~a_ & b_).sum()), int((~a_ & ~b_).sum())]], exact=False, correction=True)
prod_ids = win.product_id.values; up = np.unique(prod_ids); idx_by_p = {p: np.where(prod_ids == p)[0] for p in up}
diffs = []
for _ in range(500):
    ii = np.concatenate([idx_by_p[p] for p in RNG.choice(up, len(up))]); diffs.append(a_[ii].mean() - b_[ii].mean())
pres = o.groupby("comp_id").related.max().values == 1
lo_o, hi_o = proportion_confint(int(oop.sum()), n_c, method="wilson")
print(f"Competitions: {n_c:,} | related present in {pres.mean():.1%} | related wins {rel_win.mean():.2%} overall and {rel_win[pres].mean():.2%} when present (documented India: 87.74%)")
print(f"Out of position: related wins without the lowest total price in {oop.mean():.2%} of competitions (CI {lo_o:.2%} to {hi_o:.2%}); documented 29.15%")
print(f"Share of related wins that were not the cheapest: {oop.sum() / rel_win.sum():.1%} | mean price gap of related winners {win[win.related == 1].gap_pct.mean():.1f}% (median {win[win.related == 1].gap_pct.median():.1f}%)")
print(f"Lowest-price rule would give related sellers {rel_lp.mean():.2%} of wins: difference {a_.mean() - b_.mean():+.2%} (bootstrap by product CI {np.percentile(diffs, 2.5):+.2%} to {np.percentile(diffs, 97.5):+.2%}); McNemar p = {mc.pvalue:.2g} (related-only wins {int((a_ & ~b_).sum()):,}, lowest-price-only {int((~a_ & b_).sum()):,})")
ws = win.seller_id.value_counts().reindex(range(len(sellers)), fill_value=0).values; lps = lp.seller_id.value_counts().reindex(range(len(sellers)), fill_value=0).values
def gini(x):
    s = np.sort(np.asarray(x, float)); n = len(s); return 1 - 2 * ((s.cumsum() - s / 2) / s.sum()).sum() / n
print(f"Concentration of wins: top-2 sellers take {np.sort(ws)[-2:].sum() / n_c:.1%} (lowest-price rule: {np.sort(lps)[-2:].sum() / n_c:.1%}) | Gini over all {len(sellers):,} sellers {gini(ws):.3f} (lowest-price rule {gini(lps):.3f}) | sellers that ever win {int((ws > 0).sum()):,} (lowest-price rule {int((lps > 0).sum()):,})")
bb.round(4)

#%% md
**Observation:** «O71»

### 7.2 Recovering the hidden rule: a conditional logit audit

**(a) Why?** An auditor sees many competitions and one winner in each. The natural model is a **conditional (fixed-effect) logit**: within each competition, the winner is the offer with the highest latent score, and the coefficients show how each feature moves that score. Two questions: (1) which features matter, in "price-gap equivalents"; (2) is there a **related-seller effect beyond the visible features** (H2)? Because we simulated the rule, we can compare estimates with the truth (rule weight divided by the noise scale).

**(b) Assumptions:** the score is linear in the chosen features; noise is Gumbel (logit); the auditor observes the same features the customer sees. We use 5,000 randomly chosen competitions. A caution: "related" is only 2 sellers with by far the largest rating counts, so scale and related status are hard to separate; we show a model without the related flag to see what happens.

#### Code

#%% code
cid = RNG.choice(comps.comp_id.values, 5000, replace=False); d = o[o.comp_id.isin(cid)].copy(); d["pos10"] = d.pos_feedback_reported / 10
def fit_cl(cols):
    return ConditionalLogit(d.is_buy_box.values, d[cols].astype(float).values, groups=d.comp_id.values).fit(disp=0)
cols_full = ["gap_pct", "fast", "pos10", "log_n", "related"]; m_full = fit_cl(cols_full); m_nor = fit_cl(cols_full[:-1])
truth = {"gap_pct": -HID["w_price"] / 100 / HID["noise"], "fast": HID["w_fast"] / HID["noise"], "pos10": HID["w_pos"] * 0.1 / HID["noise"], "log_n": HID["w_logn"] / HID["noise"], "related": HID["w_related"] / HID["noise"]}
ci = m_full.conf_int(); tab = pd.DataFrame({"coef (with related flag)": m_full.params, "SE": m_full.bse, "CI low": ci[:, 0], "CI high": ci[:, 1], "p": m_full.pvalues, "hidden truth": [truth[c] for c in cols_full]}, index=cols_full)
tab["coef (without related flag)"] = list(m_nor.params) + [np.nan]
V = m_full.cov_params(); b1, b2 = m_full.params[4], m_full.params[0]; r = b1 / abs(b2); se_r = abs(r) * np.sqrt(V[4, 4] / b1 ** 2 + V[0, 0] / b2 ** 2 - 2 * V[0, 4] / (b1 * b2) * (-1) * (-1))
r_logn = m_full.params[3] / abs(b2); r_true = truth["related"] / abs(truth["gap_pct"])
print(f"Conditional logit on {d.comp_id.nunique():,} competitions ({len(d):,} offers). Wald test of related = 0: z = {b1 / m_full.bse[4]:.1f}, p = {m_full.pvalues[4]:.2g}")
print(f"Price-gap equivalents: related status ≈ {r:.1f} pct points of price gap (approx. SE {se_r:.1f}; hidden truth {r_true:.1f}) | 10x more lifetime ratings ≈ {r_logn:.1f} pct points (truth {truth['log_n'] / abs(truth['gap_pct']):.1f}) | fast delivery ≈ {m_full.params[1] / abs(b2):.1f} (truth {truth['fast'] / abs(truth['gap_pct']):.1f})")
print(f"Without the related flag the log-ratings coefficient moves from {m_full.params[3]:.2f} to {m_nor.params[3]:.2f} (truth {truth['log_n']:.2f}): scale absorbs the related effect")
tab.round(3)

#%% md
**Observation:** «O72»

### 7.3 Do customers agree? First-preference votes in the top-4 survey

**(a) Why?** The audit shows participants the top-4 offers for 60 products where a related seller **won the box without the lowest price**, with every feature visible, and records their first preference. If the algorithm's pick were also the customers' pick, the related share would be near 100% in these tasks. Each participant votes 15 times, so votes are **clustered by participant**: we use Wilson CIs, a **participant-level cluster bootstrap**, the **ICC** and the **design effect**. A second identical control group acts as a replication (placebo) check.

**(b) Assumptions:** respondents are independent of each other; tasks are a random draw of 15 from 60.

#### Code

#%% code
evs = survey[survey.is_related == 1].copy()
def arm_stats(arm, B=1000):
    x = evs[evs.arm == arm]; k, n = int(x.chosen.sum()), len(x); lo, hi = proportion_confint(k, n, method="wilson")
    pp = x.groupby("participant_id").chosen.agg(["sum", "count"]); bs = []
    for _ in range(B):
        ii = RNG.integers(0, len(pp), len(pp)); bs.append(pp["sum"].values[ii].sum() / pp["count"].values[ii].sum())
    msb = 15 * ((pp["sum"] / 15 - k / n) ** 2).sum() / (len(pp) - 1); msw = ((x.groupby("participant_id").chosen.apply(lambda v: ((v - v.mean()) ** 2).sum())).sum()) / (n - len(pp))
    icc = (msb - msw) / (msb + 14 * msw); return k, n, k / n, lo, hi, np.percentile(bs, 2.5), np.percentile(bs, 97.5), icc
s1 = arm_stats("S1_control"); t0c = arm_stats("T0_control"); icc_c = np.mean([s1[7], t0c[7]]); deff = 1 + 14 * max(icc_c, 0)
pb = stats.binomtest(s1[0], s1[1], 0.25)
cheap = survey[survey.arm == "S1_control"].copy(); cheap["is_cheapest"] = cheap.groupby(["participant_id", "task_comp_id"]).price_premium_pct.transform("min") == cheap.price_premium_pct
cheap_share = cheap[(cheap.chosen == 1)].is_cheapest.mean()
cc = smf.logit("chosen ~ C(arm)", data=evs[evs.arm.isin(["S1_control", "T0_control"])]).fit(disp=0, cov_type="cluster", cov_kwds={"groups": evs[evs.arm.isin(["S1_control", "T0_control"])].participant_id.astype("category").cat.codes})
print(f"S1 control: {s1[0]} of {s1[1]} evaluations chose the related offer = {s1[2]:.1%} (Wilson CI {s1[3]:.1%} to {s1[4]:.1%}; cluster bootstrap {s1[5]:.1%} to {s1[6]:.1%}); documented: 407 of 750 = 54.3%")
print(f"Algorithm picked the related offer in 100% of these 60 tasks by construction; gap to customers {1 - s1[2]:.1%}. Binomial test against chance (25%): p = {pb.pvalue:.2g}")
print(f"Customers picked the cheapest offer in {cheap_share:.1%} of evaluations | ICC (S1, T0) = {s1[7]:.3f}, {t0c[7]:.3f}; design effect for 15 tasks per person = {deff:.2f}")
print(f"Replication: second control {t0c[2]:.1%} (CI {t0c[3]:.1%} to {t0c[4]:.1%}); difference vs S1 {t0c[2] - s1[2]:+.1%}, cluster-robust p = {cc.pvalues.iloc[1]:.2g}")
pd.DataFrame([("S1_control",) + s1[:7], ("T0_control",) + t0c[:7]], columns=["group", "related chosen", "evaluations", "share", "Wilson low", "Wilson high", "boot low", "boot high"]).set_index("group").round(4)

