### 7.4 What drives customer choice? A random-forest surrogate, checked against a logit

**(a) Why?** As in the audit, a **random forest** on the first-preference votes ranks the features customers use (price premium, fast delivery, average rating, % positive feedback, number of ratings). The forest is a descriptive surrogate of respondents, **not** of the platform's algorithm. We pool the two control groups, evaluate on **held-out participants** (GroupKFold) and use **permutation importance** on the held-out folds (impurity importance is biased towards high-cardinality features). A **conditional logit** on the same data is the cross-check, and since we know the hidden utility we can compare both with the truth (standardised effect = |weight| × feature SD).

**(b) Assumptions:** forest with fixed defaults (200 trees, min leaf 20), no tuning; respondents are one pooled population.

#### Code

#%% code
sc = survey[survey.arm.isin(["S1_control", "T0_control"])].copy(); sc["log_n"] = np.log10(sc.n_ratings_shown); sc["eval_id"] = sc.participant_id + "_" + sc.task_comp_id.astype(str)
feat = ["price_premium_pct", "fast_delivery", "avg_rating_shown", "pos_pct_shown", "log_n"]; Xs, ys, gs = sc[feat].values, sc.chosen.values, sc.participant_id.values
imp = np.zeros(len(feat)); oof = np.zeros(len(sc)); impurity = np.zeros(len(feat))
for tr, te in GroupKFold(5).split(Xs, ys, gs):
    rf = RandomForestRegressor(200, min_samples_leaf=20, random_state=SEED, n_jobs=-1).fit(Xs[tr], ys[tr]); oof[te] = rf.predict(Xs[te]); impurity += rf.feature_importances_ / 5
    imp += permutation_importance(rf, Xs[te], ys[te], n_repeats=5, random_state=SEED, scoring="r2").importances_mean / 5
sc["pred"] = oof; top1 = sc.loc[sc.groupby("eval_id").pred.idxmax()].chosen.mean(); r2 = r2_score(ys, oof)
cl = ConditionalLogit(sc.chosen.values, sc[feat].astype(float).values, groups=sc.eval_id.values).fit(disp=0)
tw = {"price_premium_pct": abs(HID["b_price"]), "fast_delivery": abs(HID["b_fast"]), "avg_rating_shown": abs(HID["b_rating"]), "pos_pct_shown": abs(HID["b_pos"]) / 10, "log_n": abs(HID["b_logn"])}
true_eff = pd.Series({f: tw[f] * sc[f].std() for f in feat}); logit_eff = pd.Series(np.abs(cl.params) * sc[feat].std().values, index=feat)
imp_tab = pd.DataFrame({"RF permutation importance (held-out)": imp, "RF impurity importance": impurity, "logit standardised effect": logit_eff.values, "hidden true standardised effect": true_eff.values}, index=feat)
imp_tab["RF rank"] = imp_tab.iloc[:, 0].rank(ascending=False); imp_tab["true rank"] = imp_tab.iloc[:, 3].rank(ascending=False)
print(f"Held-out R² of the forest = {r2:.3f}; it picks the voted option first in {top1:.1%} of {sc.eval_id.nunique():,} evaluations (chance 25%)")
print(f"Spearman(RF permutation rank, true rank) = {stats.spearmanr(imp_tab['RF rank'], imp_tab['true rank'])[0]:+.2f}; logit coefficient on log10(ratings) = {cl.params[4]:.2f} (hidden {HID['b_logn']:.2f})")
imp_tab.round(4)

#%% code
fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(13, 4.2)); lab = ["price premium", "fast delivery", "avg rating", "% positive", "log10 ratings"]
axes[0].barh(lab, imp, color="#2a6f97"); axes[0].set_xlabel("Held-out permutation importance (drop in R²)"); axes[0].set_ylabel("Feature shown to respondents"); axes[0].set_title("Random-forest surrogate: what customers use")
axes[1].barh(lab, true_eff.values, color="#e07a1f"); axes[1].set_xlabel("Hidden true standardised effect on utility"); axes[1].set_ylabel("Feature shown to respondents"); axes[1].set_title("Hidden truth in the simulation")
plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()

