**Takeaway:** «O78»

## 8. ML Section — a surrogate of the winner rule and what it can and cannot tell an auditor

An outside auditor can also train a **classifier on offers** that predicts which offer wins, and read the **feature ablation**: how much does top-1 accuracy (picking the actual winner in each competition) fall when a feature group is removed? The model is a **gradient-boosted classifier** with defaults, evaluated out-of-fold with folds grouped by competition. It describes the rule; it does not reveal it, and it cannot separate a related-seller bonus from scale when the two move together.

#%% code
from sklearn.ensemble import HistGradientBoostingClassifier as HGB
sets = {"all features": ["gap_pct", "fast", "pos_feedback_reported", "log_n", "avg_rating_reported", "related"], "without related flag": ["gap_pct", "fast", "pos_feedback_reported", "log_n", "avg_rating_reported"],
        "without related flag and ratings count": ["gap_pct", "fast", "pos_feedback_reported", "avg_rating_reported"], "price and delivery only": ["gap_pct", "fast"]}
ml_rows = []; yb = o.is_buy_box.values; gcomp = o.comp_id.values
for k, f_ in sets.items():
    p = np.zeros(len(o))
    for tr, te in GroupKFold(5).split(o, yb, gcomp):
        p[te] = HGB(max_iter=150, random_state=SEED).fit(o.iloc[tr][f_], yb[tr]).predict_proba(o.iloc[te][f_])[:, 1]
    tmp = o[["comp_id", "is_buy_box"]].copy(); tmp["p"] = p; top = tmp.loc[tmp.groupby("comp_id").p.idxmax()].is_buy_box.mean(); ml_rows.append((k, roc_auc_score(yb, p), top))
ml = pd.DataFrame(ml_rows, columns=["feature set", "AUC (out-of-fold)", "top-1 accuracy per competition"]).set_index("feature set")
ml.loc["baseline: lowest price wins"] = [np.nan, (lp.is_buy_box == 1).mean()]; ml.loc["baseline: related if present, else lowest price"] = [np.nan, np.mean(np.where(pres, rel_win, lp.is_buy_box.values == 1))]
print(f"Offers {len(o):,} | winners per competition 1 | random pick would be right about {(1 / o.groupby('comp_id').size()).mean():.1%} of the time")
ml.round(4)

#%% md
**Observation:** «O8»

## 9. Business Translation — ₹ impact

The platform adopts the **quality-normalised rule with rotation** (rule 4) in place of the current rule. Assumptions (D-30-07): **20 lakh Buy Box orders a day**; platform commission **8%** of the value customers spend; a defective order (refund, return pickup, support contact) costs the platform **₹400** (range ₹250 to ₹600); per-order effects are the paired bootstrap results of 7.8. Customer savings are a transfer to customers, not platform revenue; the platform's own effect is the defect cost avoided minus commission lost on lower prices. Not valued: seller-trust and regulatory risk, effects on sellers' pricing and on conversion.

#### Code

#%% code
ORD, COMM = 20e5, 0.08; k4 = "4 quality-normalised + rotation"; dp, dd_ = cmp_[k4]["Δ overpay ₹"], cmp_[k4]["Δ defect rate (pts)"]
def yr(dpay_, ddef_pts, cost):
    cust = -dpay_ * ORD * 365 / 1e7; defects = -ddef_pts / 100 * ORD * 365 * cost / 1e7; comm = dpay_ * ORD * 365 * COMM / 1e7; return cust, defects, comm, defects + comm
sc_rows = {"Best": yr(dp[1], dd_[1], 600), "Base": yr(dp[0], dd_[0], 400), "Worst": yr(dp[2], dd_[2], 250)}
money = pd.DataFrame(sc_rows, index=["customer saving (₹ crore / year)", "defect cost avoided (₹ crore / year)", "commission effect of lower prices (₹ crore / year)", "net platform effect (₹ crore / year)"]).T
print(f"Per-order effects (rule 4 vs current): overpay {dp[0]:+.1f} ₹ (CI {dp[1]:+.1f} to {dp[2]:+.1f}); defect rate {dd_[0]:+.3f} pts (CI {dd_[1]:+.3f} to {dd_[2]:+.3f})")
print("Scenarios: Best = favourable end of both CIs and ₹600 per defect; Base = point estimates and ₹400; Worst = unfavourable ends and ₹250")
money.round(2)

