«O10»

## 11. Risks, Limitations and Next Steps

- **Simulated world:** the winner rule, the strike-through process and the survey utilities are assumptions calibrated to the audit's figures; all conclusions about *Amazon* are about the audit's documented numbers, not about Amazon's system. Ground-truth checks (7.2, 7.4) show what an audit can and cannot recover **in this simulation**.
- **Identification limit:** a related-seller preference cannot be cleanly separated from scale when two sellers have far more ratings than anyone else. An external audit should say so, and a platform should audit internally with randomisation (hold-out rules).
- **Survey realism:** respondents saw only four offers on a static screen and chose after being told the task; real customers click the box. The treatment shares are sensitive to utility weights that we calibrated, so the replication of the documented shares is by construction, not independent evidence.
- **No strategic response:** the redesign assumes sellers keep prices and quality; real sellers will respond, so a staged A/B or a shadow-ranking period is needed.
- **Data the outsider does not have:** defect and late-shipment rates exist only inside the platform; the redesign in 7.8 uses them and so is a platform-side exercise.
- **Next steps:** run a shadow ranker for four weeks on real competitions; audit rotation and win concentration by seller size monthly; publish the rectified metric; test the rule with a randomised holdout of products; examine the same pattern for Flipkart and Meesho once data exist.

## 12. Appendix

| Quantity | Formula |
|---|---|
| Price gap | gap % = (price + shipping) / (lowest price + shipping) − 1, × 100 |
| Out of position | related seller wins while the lowest-total-price offer is another seller's |
| Conditional logit | P(offer j wins in competition c) = exp(x_j'β) / Σ_k exp(x_k'β) |
| Price-gap equivalent | β_feature / |β_gap| (percentage points of price gap one unit of the feature is worth) |
| Rectified feedback | positive reviews / all reviews (struck-through included) |
| Reported feedback | positive reviews / (all reviews − struck-through reviews) |
| Shrunk feedback | (positive + 30 × pool mean) / (reviews + 30) |
| ICC / design effect | ICC = (MSB − MSW) / (MSB + (m − 1) MSW); deff = 1 + (m − 1) ICC |
| Cohen's h | 2 asin√p₁ − 2 asin√p₂ |
| Gini of wins | inequality of Buy Box wins across all sellers |
| McNemar | test on competitions where exactly one of two rules gives a related-seller win |

| Term | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Buy Box | The default "Add to Cart" offer on a multi-seller product page |
| Related seller | Marketplace-owned or marketplace-linked special merchant (Cloudtail, Appario in the audit) |
| FBA | Fulfilled by the marketplace: the platform stores, packs and ships |
| Out of position | Winning the box without the lowest price |
| Strike-through | Removing a low-rated review from a seller's displayed metrics |
| Rectified metric | The metric recomputed with all reviews |
| Surrogate model | A model fitted to observed behaviour to explain it, not the real algorithm |
| Rotation | Sharing the box among near-equal eligible sellers |
